    {
      "id": "GCR-03",
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "anchor": "lifts-gram-column-product",
      "title": "The product formula cancels the cross-coordinate normalizations",
      "statement": "For t∈F× one has ∏_v c_v(t)^n=1. The standard lattice fibre mass also splits into the first-vector and complement masses away from finitely many places.",
      "hypotheses": "The factors use normalized local absolute values of F, and c_v(t)=|t|_v^{-2} equals one almost everywhere. In global strict-rank recursion n+1≤r and either r≥2 or the full ambient space ⟨t⟩⊕J is anisotropic.",
      "correspondence": "The two local changes of Haar need not be one individually. Their product is one before multiplying the separate standard-lattice tails. The worked example takes t=2 in Q(i)/Q.",
      "dependencies": [
        "GCR-02"
      ],
      "equations": [],
